#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8533b05d-9d83-5831-96d2-db44a0893160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250783} {:answer/id #uuid "1a82083f-f98e-5cce-b156-d399c04e9fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250784} {:answer/id #uuid "97976dcc-cb37-5996-a487-12b0edec9650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250785} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a74f5a-ee55-52a6-bd3f-898bcefda788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250786} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8c142b-7970-5a39-a6ca-538985700414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8c142b-7970-5a39-a6ca-538985700414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250787} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad01743-5cc8-5c57-9ff7-92a224f1d59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb1887b-828c-58c3-9b10-3c0e7dc6d40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124965" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/cd41c969aee2e7c04c9034106978d8522d6a715733098bfde87abbdb861da7a8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1452c68-9d3f-5dc9-a072-c115505e2575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250790} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfd53c5-c758-5f5e-a88b-a8c1cdba0cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250791} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1a24a2-a304-5887-bbb6-ee7912794c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250792} {:answer/id #uuid "5722a1c9-33f5-5a48-b8d6-8c17151c3bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250793} {:answer/id #uuid "53e3e557-fc58-535c-9dfe-f69c3dcb716a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =10\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1452c68-9d3f-5dc9-a072-c115505e2575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250790} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccaadd9-6ac4-5d88-aae6-d67d2afc2a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4051fa04-8ea2-56f8-9836-0af07938d8b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125000" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/82ad5587912af9d07bf447281bba3eb714bfdfdf38fef529529956912b0a77ec.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{9}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{81}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{19}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10})}=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{19}}=\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abab664d-871f-55d3-857b-8e9ad1ca3413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250797} {:answer/id #uuid "909a83a9-601f-5a47-8f39-0ed33345b241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{12\\sqrt{10}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250798} {:answer/id #uuid "5e54d5d8-97f3-5e5f-b0ce-89811800aa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250799} {:answer/id #uuid "056edd12-572f-5501-b084-fa4c184b84df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250800} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfd33c5-f0d6-5dee-a4b5-fa881f8803c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{10}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056edd12-572f-5501-b084-fa4c184b84df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250800} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1034edd-59cf-56be-bb6e-dd2bc8dea1e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7e5b456-31be-5adc-bcb7-b8fe111eed32" :question/math-academy-id "q-24069" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ and ${0}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/9d9aa1e6ffac387c53c7a1b35217a51cd17db7242ddedec4ff72c229a73d5291.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3c859f-6933-51c1-bd20-19461ae114af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250804} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dd8e5f-61aa-5df4-8ebb-2ab41b937710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250805} {:answer/id #uuid "620b7d59-d337-5de3-ad58-638df86207d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250806} {:answer/id #uuid "a56d5efc-5ccf-5f34-8513-e254bcf90ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250807} {:answer/id #uuid "541bed6c-d5a5-5d0f-90f9-da5c1f478b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dd8e5f-61aa-5df4-8ebb-2ab41b937710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250805} :answer-field/id #uuid "37911eeb-03d0-56e2-9a45-bc722d11ff1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa952d8d-3630-5d49-a36f-eb08d27d8910" :question/math-academy-id "q-45875" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e0c03b-cd67-5be0-930f-38b8eba48b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250811} {:answer/id #uuid "b36ccd70-8f5e-52fa-98e3-7c33cf234fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250812} {:answer/id #uuid "ac623ac3-6967-57be-b8b5-a6d40ac9899d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250813} {:answer/id #uuid "79ae95b9-7c72-5cfd-9222-3fa1ec2dc50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250814} {:answer/id #uuid "cb348247-2f45-5e6e-891f-94d7f4605b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb348247-2f45-5e6e-891f-94d7f4605b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250815} :answer-field/id #uuid "7842503d-cd31-55da-baad-eb8ec6a7f421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250810}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7610e488-9f30-5416-a6f7-4b910f43ed02" :question/math-academy-id "q-85646" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/dcbdf7c41968a054b4fd2f3e761685d6ea9a5bf1677ec1c15db25464f1d1039d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\frac{7}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/38a739ca964d1e9cf343914ed0a66f58363ceb7acfe7c194daf2765c0b4bf4d5.png)\n\nWe're given that $x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7}{9}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{32}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9})}=-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\n$$"}]}
